BT /F2 18 Tf 48 762 Td (Offer Extraction Pipeline: Solution Overview) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 738 Td (Turning scraped AT&T iPhone offer data \(JSON\) into a structured 29-column CSV, using an LLM) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 725 Td (plus plain Python rules.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 697 Td (1. The problem I was given) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 674 Td (I started with a JSON file holding 12 iPhone products. For each one I had the basics, name,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 661 Td (brand, storage variant, price, stock and shipment, plus a list of promotional offers written as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 648 Td (loose, unstructured text. Bill credits, switcher rewards, trade-in deals and credit card offers) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 635 Td (were all mixed in there.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 610 Td (The output had to be a CSV with 29 fixed columns. And every product had to show up twice:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 60 585 Td (- Row 1: the customer is adding a New Line) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 60 566 Td (- Row 2: the customer is doing an Upgrade) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 541 Td (That second part mattered more than it first looked. The same offer can count for one line type) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 528 Td (and not the other, so I could not just copy a product's offers into both rows. Each row had to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 515 Td (be worked out on its own.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 487 Td (2. How I set it up) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 464 Td (In short, the pipeline runs in six steps:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 439 Td (Input.json, then load the data and copy the basic fields straight across \(no LLM\), then gather) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 426 Td (the offer texts and drop the duplicates, then run the LLM extraction with Gemini \(structured) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 413 Td (JSON output\), then check that LLM output, then apply the Python rules for line type, variant) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 400 Td (and column mapping, then write output.csv, 24 rows by 29 columns.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 375 Td (I kept the LLM's job deliberately small. It only reads and classifies the offer text. Python) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 362 Td (decides which value goes into which row and column. I did that on purpose: if something looks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 349 Td (wrong in the CSV, I can trace it back to a rule I wrote, rather than guessing what a model was) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 336 Td (thinking.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 48 308 Td (3. What each step actually does) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 48 284 Td (Copying the basic fields) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 262 Td (Product name, brand, variant, MSRP, plan type, stock and shipment come straight from the JSON) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 249 Td (keys. No model involved, nothing to interpret.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 48 223 Td (Gathering the offer texts) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 201 Td (For each offer I joined the title, description, modal text and card legal text into one block) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 188 Td (of text, and gave each block a short hash ID. The 12 products hold 48 offers in total, but a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 175 Td (lot of them repeat. Only 11 of those texts are unique, so I only needed 11 LLM calls. That) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 162 Td (saved a fair bit of time and quota.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 48 136 Td (Letting Gemini read the offers) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 114 Td (Each unique text goes to Gemini with a Pydantic schema, so the model has to return valid JSON.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 48 101 Td (I set temperature to 0 to keep the output as steady as possible. For each offer it returns:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 60 76 Td (- category: device discount, service line, trade-in, switcher or finance) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 60 57 Td (- eligible line types: New Line, Upgrade, or both) Tj ET
endstream
